{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Six cities of refuge shall be yours.</b> This teaches that even though Moses set apart three cities in his lifetime, they did not function as places of refuge until three were set apart in the land of Canaan. And even though in the land of Canaan there were nine and a half tribes, while here there are only two and a half, Scripture equalized the number of their cities of refuge — for in Gilead murderers are common, as it says (Hosea 6:8): ״גלעד קרית פועלי און עקובה מדם״. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 25:8): ״טוב וישר ה׳ על כן יורה חטאים בדרך״ — if to murderers He gives a path and a road to flee and escape, then for the righteous how much the more so." "<b>And the Ramban</b>, of blessed memory, gave two reasons for why Scripture multiplied cities of refuge across the Jordan as in all the land of Canaan: the first, for the honor of Moses who set them apart; and the second, because the land of Sihon and Og, which was across the Jordan, was exceedingly great, for its kings were mighty, whereas the land of Canaan, even though there were thirty-one kings in it, they were not kings of countries but rather of places and cities close to one another, a half-day's journey between some of them — as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded concerning the excellence of the land, that all the kings wanted to have an inheritance in the land and to have their names called upon it; therefore Transjordan was worthy of three cities of refuge like all the land of Canaan."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:58.410242384Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}